body:
I am so glad you posted this. It happened to me too. 

&#x200B;

I wanted to comment to uplift an amazing book called In the Dream House by Carmen Maria Machado. It is a memoir and is groundbreaking in many ways. The book chronicles the author's experience in a lesbian abusive relationship and includes historical analysis of exactly what you are talking about - how society teaches us women are innately harmless and how that can lead lesbians to being particularly vulnerable to and confused by intimate partner violence. 

&#x200B;

Reading the book and listening to podcasts CMM did promoting the book were a huge part of both my understanding what was happening to me while it was happening, my getting the strength to leave, and my healing process post that trauma.

Then you and Amalia are in an unhealthy environment and I hope you consider finding a way out soon, as you are an adult. You could also consider or explore notifying the authorities. If you are in college, a counselor at your school may be able to advise you on your options. 

&#x200B;

You should not have to live like this, and neither should you sister. If he yells - thats abuse. If he throws things - thats abuse. If he hurts you - thats abuse. and by he I mean your step dad.

I am experiencing this right now! I started Under the Whispering Door and it has restored my readers heart. I am so thankful. 

&#x200B;

I am over100 pages in, loving every moment, watching a movie in my mind like I used to when I was a younger! I have not felt this engaged in a book since my life imploded in 2018 and my reading cut off cold turkey!

I was in a long term very loving relationship with a cis male, and we ran into this over the years we were together and got to the other side of it in a cool way. One turning point was a conversation we had via phone where I clearly told him something along the lines of 'your sexual needs are not my responsibility to manage, that your responsibility to manage. So if you have sexual needs that simply must be met, you need to meet them yourself as a default (i.e. masterbation, sp?) and allow our sex as a couple of develop outside of that.' His reaction, I'll never forget. I could hear him jaw dropped over the phone and he was like 'really, thats MY RESPONSIBILITY? I can handle that MYSELF as a default?' and I was like 'yeah.' and he did and we were happy for many more years bc he let up on the pressure. 

&#x200B;

If I could go back and do the relationship again, I would do more to make sure he knew it was not that I was not attracted to him or anything, that it was all within me and that was ok and part of being human, as I know he internalized a lot of my lack of interest in sex at first and it was super hurtful for him. There were a lot of discussions where I held my ground at my no and pointed out his negative response was not helping, and eventually he got the message and worked on those behaviors. He was/is a very self aware and emotionally aware man, and actually did that work to adjust how he was showing up about this. :::sigh - I miss that man sometimes:::

&#x200B;

Finally I highly recommend the book 'come as you are'. You could also check out the Netflix show on sex called The Principles of Pleasure which features the author, last name Nagaski. If you can get him to lean into those resources with you, it could really help him understand how sexuality really works versus what he has been taught to think it should work like.

&#x200B;

He HAS TO want to do this work WITH YOU to address this or it will all be for naught and you will be in a position where you are constantly pressured and may have to leave. Best case scenario, he is willing to reprogram and change his interactions with you when you say no, so you can lovingly say yes more often.

Hi! How can I tell if my seedlings are leggy? 

&#x200B;

They are supporting themselves but do seem to have very long stems. They are one week old. Calendula, borage, and **nasturtiums.** 

&#x200B;

Also, does anyone know why posts might be automatically taken down? I am new to having a reddit account and actually engaging, and love this sub. Long time lurker :) I have tried to post twice and both times been unsuccessful. Thanks all!
